course: history
lesson: 35
title: Space: from Sputnik to the Moon
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: After the Second World War the USA and the Soviet Union became rivals in the Cold War, and they raced to be first in space, like two classmates each trying to finish the science project first.
explain: In 1957 the Soviet Union launched Sputnik, a beeping metal ball that was the first artificial satellite, and its rockets were designed by Serhiy Korolev, born in Zhytomyr in Ukraine.
explain: In 1961 Yuri Gagarin became the first person in space, in 1963 Valentina Tereshkova the first woman, and on 20 July 1969 the Americans Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin walked on the Moon.
explain: Their footprints are still there, because the Moon has no wind or rain to blow them away.
explain: Behind every astronaut were thousands of people on Earth, and today the old rivals often work together, with astronauts from many countries living on the International Space Station.
fact: After the Second World War the USA and the Soviet Union were rivals in the Cold War and raced to be first in space.
fact: In 1957 the Soviet Union launched Sputnik, the first artificial satellite, a metal ball that beeped as it circled the Earth.
fact: The chief designer of the Soviet rockets was Serhiy Korolev, who was born in Zhytomyr in Ukraine.
fact: Also in 1957, a month after Sputnik, a dog called Laika became the first animal to orbit the Earth, and she did not survive the flight.
fact: On 12 April 1961 Yuri Gagarin became the first person in space, orbiting the Earth once in 108 minutes.
fact: In 1963 Valentina Tereshkova became the first woman in space.
fact: On 20 July 1969 the American astronauts Neil Armstrong and Buzz Aldrin landed on the Moon in Apollo 11 while Michael Collins circled above.
fact: Armstrong said: That's one small step for man, one giant leap for mankind, and about 600 million people watched on television.
fact: The astronauts collected Moon rocks and left a flag and footprints, which are still there because there is no wind on the Moon.
fact: Twelve people walked on the Moon between 1969 and 1972.
fact: Thousands of people worked on Apollo, including the mathematician Katherine Johnson and the programmer Margaret Hamilton.
fact: In 1970 an oxygen tank exploded on Apollo 13 on the way to the Moon, and the crew and mission control worked out how to bring them home safely.
fact: Today astronauts from many countries live on the International Space Station, robots explore Mars, and in 1997 Leonid Kadenyuk became the first astronaut of independent Ukraine.
exercise: Which two countries raced to be first in space? | The USA and the Soviet Union | Britain and France, the old allies | China and Japan | Germany and the United Kingdom
exercise: What was Sputnik? | The first artificial satellite | The first rocket to reach the Moon | A Soviet cosmonaut | The first space station in orbit
exercise: In which year was Sputnik launched? | 1957 | 1969 | 1945 | 2000
exercise: Who designed the Soviet rockets? | Serhiy Korolev | Cosmonaut Yuri Gagarin | Wernher von Braun | Valentina Tereshkova
exercise: Who was Laika? | The first animal to orbit the Earth | The first woman to fly into space | A Soviet rocket | The first dog to walk on the Moon
exercise: Who was the first person in space? | Yuri Gagarin | Neil Armstrong | Buzz Aldrin of Apollo 11 | Valentina Tereshkova
exercise: On which date did Gagarin fly into space? | 12 April 1961 | 20 July 1969 | 4 October 1957 | 6 June 1944
exercise: How long did Gagarin's flight around the Earth take? | 108 minutes | 108 days | 10 hours | 8 minutes
exercise: Who was the first woman in space? | Valentina Tereshkova | Marie Curie | Katherine Johnson | Margaret Hamilton
exercise: Who first walked on the Moon? | Neil Armstrong | Cosmonaut Yuri Gagarin | Michael Collins | Laika the space dog
exercise: When did Apollo 11 land on the Moon? | 20 July 1969 | 12 April 1961 | 1 January 2000 | 25 December 1957
exercise: Why are the astronauts' footprints still on the Moon? | There is no wind or rain to wipe them away | A team of guards from NASA keeps watch over them all the time | They were pressed into wet concrete | They are not there any more
exercise: How many people walked on the Moon between 1969 and 1972? | Twelve | Only two, on Apollo 11 | About one hundred | None at all, it was faked
exercise: Who was Katherine Johnson? | A mathematician who worked on the space flights | The first woman to walk on the surface of the Moon | A Soviet cosmonaut | A famous dog in space
exercise: What happened on Apollo 13? | An oxygen tank exploded, but the crew got home safely | It became the very first spaceship to land safely on Mars | The crew stayed on the Moon for a year | It was the first Moon landing
exercise: Where do astronauts from many countries live today? | The International Space Station | On a base on the far side of the Moon | On Mars | Inside the old Sputnik
test: dude says the moon landing was faked in a studio. what do u say | It was real; hundreds of millions watched and astronauts brought back Moon rocks | He's right, nobody went and the pictures were made in a film studio | Only the dog went | They secretly landed on Mars instead of the Moon
test: Who got into space first: a person, a dog or a satellite? | A satellite, then a dog, then a person | A person, then a dog, then a satellite | A dog, then a person, then a satellite | All on the same day
test: Mia says Armstrong was the first person in space. What is right? | Gagarin was first in space; Armstrong was first on the Moon | Armstrong was first in space and also the first to walk on the Moon | Tereshkova was first on the Moon | Laika was first on the Moon
test: Why is Apollo 13 called a successful failure? | It never landed, but calm teamwork brought the crew home | It landed on the Moon twice in one trip and won a big prize | It was a success in every way | Nobody at all was on board the ship
test: What would happen to a footprint on a sandy beach compared with one on the Moon? | The beach one washes away; the Moon one stays | Both footprints stay exactly where they are for ever and ever | Both vanish in a day | The Moon one is washed away by rain first
test: Why did one small step get called one giant leap? | It was a small step for one man but a huge achievement for people | Armstrong jumped very high off the ladder, much higher than on Earth | The surface was steep | He tripped on the ladder and fell
test: your mate says space race = russia only. what about ukraine? | The chief rocket designer Korolev was born in Ukraine | Ukraine had nothing to do with space | Ukraine built and launched the Apollo 11 rocket from Kyiv | Gagarin was the president of Ukraine
test: Gagarin went round the Earth once in 108 minutes. Roughly how long is that? | Just under two hours | About a day | About a week | About ten minutes
test: Why do rivals of the Space Race now often work together in space? | Big projects like a space station are easier when countries share the work | A law on the Moon forces every country in space to work together all the time | Space is too small for two | They never work together
test: In a cartoon a lone hero builds a rocket in his garage and flies to the Moon. What is more real? | Thousands of people worked together on Apollo | One brave person did it all on his own in a shed | Only the astronauts did any work | Rockets are easy to build at home
exam: Identify the first artificial satellite placed in orbit about the Earth. | Sputnik | Apollo 11 | The International Space Station | Laika
exam: Ascertain the identity of the first human being to journey into outer space. | Yuri Gagarin | Neil Armstrong | Valentina Tereshkova | Buzz Aldrin
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the persistence of the footprints left upon the lunar surface? | They endure owing to the absence of wind and rain upon the Moon | They were removed by subsequent missions | They have been eroded by lunar rainfall and the action of the tides | They were never made
exam: Select the response consistent with the established facts concerning the number of persons who walked upon the Moon between nineteen hundred and sixty-nine and nineteen hundred and seventy-two. | Twelve | Two | Twenty-four | One
